Open local media without turning its bytes into text.

A browser without the File System Access API cannot hand the workbench a file
handle, so both places that take a local file - the "Open Files..." fallback in
the unsupported-browser dialog, and a drop onto the workbench - fall back to a
FileReader and `extractFileListData`. That helper returns an `untitled:`
resource carrying the bytes, and an untitled editor is a text editor: the buffer
is decoded as text before any preview can read it, so a picture opens as
mojibake and only previews once it has been saved into the workspace.

The fix belongs in the helper, not in either caller: both of them hand its
result straight to `openEditors`, and fixing one leaves the other corrupting the
same file. Media keeps its bytes in the temporary in-memory filesystem and
travels as an ordinary resource, so the custom editor for its type resolves as
it would for a workspace file. Text is untouched and still opens untitled.

The temporary resource keeps the original basename under a uuid folder - the
editor selector globs on the basename and the tab shows it - and writeFile
creates that folder on its own. Nothing is added to the workspace.

